        how: "That comment is FOLKLORE, not an enforced check -- ensureThree.js only re-fetches when the files are ABSENT (a ship-strip slip or deletion), never compares versions, and nothing in the tree verifies the claim. Diffed r160 against three.js's current dev branch directly: the PUBLIC API the four viewer pages actually call is unchanged (GLTFLoader.setDRACOLoader/setKTX2Loader/setMeshoptDecoder identical; OrbitControls constructor(object, domElement) identical, only its base class moved EventDispatcher -> a new Controls class internally; both pages already use the modern renderer.outputColorSpace/texture.colorSpace API, not the deprecated r152-era encoding one). But the update is NOT a same-shape drop-in like the basis transcoder: three.module.js has been split since r160 -- it is now a thin re-export shim over a NEW required file, build/three.core.js (60,586 lines, 1.46 MB), which does not exist at r160 and is not in ensureThree.js's FILES list; the sibling vendor/three-webgpu already vendors its own three.core.js, confirming the split is real and current. OrbitControls.js also grew 1417 -> 1972 lines (+39%) and GLTFLoader.js 4663 -> 4925 (+5.6%). Separately, vendor/three/jsm/utils/BufferGeometryUtils.js is vendored and self-healed by ensureThree.js but grep finds ZERO callers anywhere outside vendor/three itself -- dead weight riding along regardless of which version ships.",
        why: "Same shape as the basis-transcoder finding, one level up: nothing FORCES this (no gate is red, no viewer has failed to load, and the APIs the tree actually touches are stable across the gap), so this is a deferred, not a bug. What is measured: updating past r160 is not a 4-file re-fetch the way ensureThree.js currently models it -- it needs three.core.js added to that FILES list and the four real viewer pages (not just a unit check) reloaded and eyeballed, because the OrbitControls base-class change and the module split are the kind of thing a byte-diff catches but a page smoke-test is what actually proves.",
