        how: "NOT a straight swap. three-bvh-csg's own README states its real limitation up front, in bold: 'due to numerical precision and corner cases resulting geometry may not be correctly completely two-manifold' -- and it points to Manifold CAD as the library to use when that guarantee actually matters. meshCSG.mjs's own header names its load-bearing invariant in exactly those terms: 'They agree on the SOLID: same volume, same watertight surface... Volume and watertightness are the invariants.' That is precisely the property three-bvh-csg admits it cannot always hold. This tree's own architecture already has the right shape to host the tradeoff rather than choose blindly between speed and correctness: the existing localized-vs-whole-wall paths are ALREADY required to agree on volume/watertightness rather than polygon-for-polygon (the gate is written that way on purpose), so a BVH-CSG fast path could sit beside the current BSP path, checked against the SAME watertightness/volume invariant already gated, rather than replacing it outright.",
        why: "This bears directly on the already-open audit item (this file's own 'meshCSG audit' entry, tracked separately as a Task): the question 'is the current CSG correctness rigorous enough' and the question 'is there a faster CSG that is LESS rigorous' are the same investigation from two directions, and answering one without the other risks either keeping a slow-but-correct implementation nobody re-examined for speed, or adopting a fast one that quietly trades away the exact property the audit was checking for.",
        upstream: "no gate is red over CSG performance today -- the localized optimization already shipped and the 318ms case is a stress scenario (12 overlapping blasts on one wall), not an observed live failure. Logged so the tradeoff is explicit before anyone reaches for the faster library on the strength of its speed claim alone.",
